          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Cauchy sequence
            is an infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            which ought to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} converge
            in the sense that successive terms get arbitrarily close together, as they would if they were getting arbitrarily close to a limit. Among sequences, only Cauchy sequences will converge; in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+complete+space"} sequentially complete space
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p The precise definition varies with the context.
          %p
            A sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i)_i
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            is
            %strong Cauchy
            if, for every positive number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            of one another. Explicitly:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \epsilon,\; \exists N,\; \forall i, j \geq N,\; |x_i - x_j| \lt \epsilon .
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i)_i
            is
            %strong Cauchy
            under the same condition, now relative to the metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            on that space. Explicitly:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \epsilon,\; \exists N,\; \forall i, j \geq N,\; d(x_i,x_j) \lt \epsilon .
          %p The same definition immediately applies to an extended quasipseudometric space (aka a Lawvere metric space), or anything in between.
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+space"} gauge space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i)_i
            is
            %strong Cauchy
            if this condition is satisfied for each gauging distance separately. Explicitly:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall d,\; \forall \epsilon,\; \exists N,\; \forall i, j \geq N,\; d(x_i,x_j) \lt \epsilon .
          %p
            In a
